@implements IDisposable
@inject InboxDesktopState State

@*
    The Inbox pane: what has been captured but not yet decided on, read the way
    a To Do app reads — lists down the side, the rows of the open list in the
    middle, the chosen row's detail on the right. The pane has no idea a backlog
    row exists: "Move to backlog" is an act the Inbox's own module performs
    through a port, and the shell hears about it afterwards through the state's
    Routed event. That is what keeps triage a decision the Inbox owns rather
    than a view of someone else's list.

    Lists and groups are the structure, and they are the reader's: a starter set
    is seeded on first start and everything after that is made, renamed, filed
    and deleted from the side menu's own context menu. The fixed "Inbox" row is
    the one list nobody made — what has been captured and not yet filed.

    Kinds are a filter, never a section. A queue of forty is read as forty rows
    newest first, and the chips above them narrow it to the videos, or the
    articles, or both. A kind is a fact about a capture; a list is where it goes.

    Three child components rather than one file, split where the state splits:
    the side menu draws lists and groups and owns the menus that change them;
    the item list draws the slice and the chips; the detail draws one item and
    the acts on it. All three read the one state object the shell registers,
    which is what lets the pane be re-mounted in another slot without
    forgetting what was open — the same bargain TasksDesktopState makes.

    The header offers the two ways something gets into the queue: Add, for a
    thing the reader has in their head right now, and Capture, for the sources
    they have asked to be watched. Add is the pane's own: the dialog's title and
    notes go through the state into the Inbox's module as an item, body and all,
    and nothing outside the Inbox learns of it. Capture is raised to the host
    and nothing more — which sources, and what a run is, belongs to the Capture
    context, and the run's outcome comes back as a sentence so the pane never
    learns that context's types either.

    The old pane took its items from the shell, which is why the shell knew what
    an inbox item looked like. This one asks its own module; the three
    parameters it does take are the Capture button's, and the shell renders it
    with those alone.
*@

<section @key=@("inbox-pane") class="inbox-pane" id="inbox-pane" aria-labelledby="inbox-pane-title" data-testid="inbox-pane">
    @* The library's section header wearing this pane's own class names, with
       the two actions in its slot. Both are drawn whether or not the queue has
       anything in it: an empty Inbox is exactly where somebody reaches for Add. *@
    <SectionHeader BaseClass="inbox-pane__header"
                   TextCssClass=""
                   EyebrowCssClass="inbox-pane__eyebrow"
                   TitleCssClass="inbox-pane__title"
                   DescriptionCssClass="inbox-pane__subtitle"
                   ActionsCssClass="inbox-pane__header-actions"
                   Eyebrow="Global pane"
                   TitleId="inbox-pane-title"
                   Title="Inbox"
                   Description="Capture, file and triage what arrived before it settles into the backlog.">
        <Actions>
            @* The id is for the dialog to hand focus back to on close; it is
               named rather than captured so it survives the header re-rendering
               while the dialog is up. *@
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                       Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                       TestId="inbox-pane-add"
                       id="inbox-pane-add"
                       OnClick="OpenAdd">Add</AppButton>
            @* Busy rather than hidden while a run is in flight, so focus survives
               the round trip and a second press cannot start a second run. *@
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                       Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                       TestId="inbox-pane-capture"
                       Busy="@CaptureRunning"
                       BusyLabel="Capturing"
                       OnClick="() => OnCapture.InvokeAsync()">Capture</AppButton>
        </Actions>
    </SectionHeader>

    @* What the last run said, wearing this pane's own class. A status rather
       than an alert: a run that found nothing new is the ordinary result, and
       the line is replaced by the next run rather than stacked. Alert renders
       nothing at all for an empty message, so there is no branch here — and
       the pane is a flex column for that reason, so an absent line leaves no
       gap behind. *@
    <Alert Message="@CaptureMessage"
           BaseClass="inbox-pane__capture-result"
           Role="status"
           TestId="inbox-pane-capture-result" />

    <div class="inbox-pane__body" data-testid="inbox-pane-body">
        <InboxSideMenu State="State" />

        @* The list is the fixed pane and the detail takes the rest: a row is a
           line of metadata that fits a known width, and a detail is a page that
           wants whatever is left. Anchored at the start for that reason. *@
        <SplitPane CssClass="inbox-pane__split"
                   Anchor="SplitPaneAnchor.Start"
                   FixedWidthRem="28"
                   MinRem="18"
                   AriaLabel="Inbox list width"
                   TestId="inbox-pane-split"
                   SeparatorTestId="inbox-pane-split-handle">
            <Start>
                <InboxItemList State="State" />
            </Start>
            <End>
                <InboxItemDetail State="State" />
            </End>
        </SplitPane>
    </div>

    @* The Add dialog: a title and, optionally, some notes. The shared Modal
       brings the focus handling and Escape; this pane brings two fields and a
       rule — nothing is filed without a title, because the module refuses an
       item without one, and a dialog that offered to try would be offering an
       error. *@
    <Modal Open="@_addOpen"
           OpenChanged="v => _addOpen = v"
           Title="Add to Inbox"
           RestoreFocusToId="inbox-pane-add"
           TestId="inbox-pane-add-dialog">
        <ChildContent>
            @* A layout wrapper for the two fields, the way the import dialog's
               form is one; the fields themselves are the library's, wrapper and
               all, so the dialog needs no styling of its own. *@
            <div class="inbox-pane__add-form">
                <TextField Label="Title"
                           Value="@_title"
                           ValueChanged="v => _title = v"
                           Id="inbox-pane-add-title"
                           TestId="inbox-pane-add-title"
                           autocomplete="off" />
                @* MarkdownEditor rather than a plain TextArea: the notes land in
                   BodyMd and the detail pane reads them back as markdown, so the
                   box they are written in should offer the same formatting every
                   other body in the product gets — a list is a button, not a
                   convention the reader has to know. Label rather than AriaLabel
                   because this stands in a form beside a labelled title. *@
                <MarkdownEditor Label="Notes"
                                Value="@_notes"
                                ValueChanged="v => _notes = v"
                                Id="inbox-pane-add-notes-input"
                                Rows="4"
                                TestId="inbox-pane-add-notes" />
            </div>
        </ChildContent>
        <Footer>
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost" OnClick="CloseAdd" TestId="inbox-pane-add-cancel">Cancel</AppButton>
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Primary"
                       Disabled="@string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_title)"
                       OnClick="SubmitAddAsync"
                       TestId="inbox-pane-add-submit">Add</AppButton>
        </Footer>
    </Modal>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>Raised when Capture is pressed. Which sources, and what a run
    /// is, the pane does not know.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnCapture { get; set; }

    /// <summary>True while the host has a run in flight; the button is busy
    /// for as long as this is.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool CaptureRunning { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the last run said, as a sentence. A string rather than a
    /// result type so the Inbox stays free of the Capture context's types — the
    /// host reads the run and writes the line.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? CaptureMessage { get; set; }

    private bool _addOpen;
    private string _title = string.Empty;
    private string _notes = string.Empty;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        State.Changed += HandleStateChanged;
    }

    private void OpenAdd() => _addOpen = true;

    private void CloseAdd()
    {
        _addOpen = false;
        _title = string.Empty;
        _notes = string.Empty;
    }

    /// <summary>Closes first, then files. The state's refusal — the only one
    /// the module has is a blank title, which the button already guards — is a
    /// toast, and the item lands selected so the detail opens on it.</summary>
    private async Task SubmitAddAsync()
    {
        // The button is disabled for a blank title, so reaching here with one
        // means the caller went around it; refusing is still cheaper than
        // asking the module to store nothing.
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_title)) return;

        var capture = new InboxCapture(_title.Trim(), _notes.Trim());
        CloseAdd();
        await State.CaptureAsync(capture);
    }

    /// <summary>The state raises from whichever thread finished the store call;
    /// the render is marshalled onto the renderer's.</summary>
    private void HandleStateChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public void Dispose()
    {
        State.Changed -= HandleStateChanged;
    }
}
